==============================================================================
 BITA CAPSTONE LAB - CONNECTION DETAILS
 Generated by Terraform. Hand each student ONE block plus the .ovpn profile.
==============================================================================

STEP 1 - GET ON THE VPN
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
VPN profile : ${ovpn_path}
VPN server  : ${vpn_endpoint}  (UDP ${vpn_port})

This is a SPLIT TUNNEL. Only the lab network (${private_subnet_cidr}) is routed
over the VPN - your normal internet traffic is untouched.

  LINUX
    sudo apt install openvpn          # or: sudo dnf install openvpn
    sudo openvpn --config ${ovpn_filename}
    # leave that terminal open; it stays in the foreground

  MACOS
    brew install openvpn
    sudo openvpn --config ${ovpn_filename}
    # or install Tunnelblick, then double-click the .ovpn file

  WINDOWS
    1. Install the OpenVPN Connect client from https://openvpn.net/client/
    2. Open it, choose "Upload File" / "Import Profile -> File"
    3. Select ${ovpn_filename}
    4. Click Connect

STEP 2 - VALIDATE THE TUNNEL
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
You should now have a tun/utun interface holding a ${vpn_client_cidr} address:

  linux/macos : ip addr show tun0     (macos: ifconfig | grep -A3 utun)
  windows     : ipconfig  ->  look for the TAP/Wintun adapter

Then ping your box (see step 3 for your IP). If the ping replies, you are in:

  ping ${first_instance_ip}          # windows: ping -n 4 ${first_instance_ip}

If ping fails, the VPN is not up - fix that before touching SSH.

STEP 3 - YOUR LAB BOX
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
${instance_blocks}

STEP 4 - GRADE YOURSELF
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The health page re-runs every check on every page load. Green check = fixed,
red X = still broken. Screenshot it when all four are green.

  Over the VPN, in a browser :  http://<your-private-ip>:8080
  In your SSH session        :  bita-check

  Prefer it literally on localhost:8080? Forward the port over SSH:
    ssh -L 8080:127.0.0.1:8080 ${admin_user}@<your-private-ip>
    then browse to http://localhost:8080

